    "notes": "If |c2/c1| grows with p, then at large p the error term X^(-1/3) dominates the secondary term X^(-1/6) in the empirical signal. This explains the apparent \u03b1_p variation 1/6 \u2192 1/3."
  },
  "headline": "Regime-transition hypothesis PARTIALLY SUPPORTED. At p=3, the c1 (secondary) term dominates (captures 88-96% of rel_dev signal) \u2014 matches DH 1/6. At p=5-11, the c2 (error) term dominates (c1 and c2 same sign). At p\u226513, the two-term fit breaks down (c1 and c2 have opposite signs, partial cancellation) \u2014 likely overfitting on only 4 buckets per prime. Full verdict: SUPPORTED for small primes, UNDERDETERMINED for large primes.",
  "honest_interpretation": {
    "p3_secondary_dominates": {
      "c1_imag": -0.568,
      "c2_imag": -0.241,
      "c1_real": -1.397,
      "c2_real": -0.221,
      "fraction_from_secondary_at_all_buckets": "88-98% across imag/real",
      "verdict": "Clean Pattern-5 reproduction of BST 2013 secondary term at p=3."
    },
    "p5_to_p11_error_term_dominates": {
      "observation": "c2 becomes larger in magnitude than c1; fraction_secondary drops to 20-66%.",
      "interpretation": "At these primes, the empirical fit is capturing more of the post-secondary error structure than the secondary term. Consistent with the regime-transition hypothesis but NOT the clean \"single-term dominates\" picture we hoped for."
    },
    "p_ge_13_model_breakdown": {
      "observation": "At p=13,17,19,23 in imag quad, c1 FLIPS SIGN to positive while c2 remains negative large. The \"fraction_from_secondary\" becomes negative or >1 (unphysical).",
      "interpretation": "With only 4 buckets per prime (the LMFDB-complete range for quadratic), a 2-parameter fit with fixed exponents (1/6, 1/3) can produce numerically unstable (c1, c2) pairs when the data is in the transition regime. The apparent sign flip is likely a least-squares artifact, not physics.",
      "symptomatic_of": "Insufficient data / model is underdetermined at large p."
    },
    "verdict": "PARTIAL SUPPORT. The regime-transition framework (BST secondary + post-secondary error) fits the p=3 data cleanly and is directionally consistent with the p=5-11 data. It BREAKS on p\u226513 due to data-limited overfitting, not due to physics contradiction. To definitively test, we need either:",
    "paths_forward": [
      "Extend |disc| coverage beyond LMFDB cutoff for more buckets \u2014 would disambiguate the 2-term fit at large primes.",
      "Use theoretical BST constants to CONSTRAIN c1 (if derivable from literature), leaving only c2 free to fit \u2014 reduces overfit risk.",
      "Include the p-dependent amplitude factor CL_asymptote(p) explicitly in the model (the 1/6 term scales with CL amplitude, which varies with p; this could explain why c1 at p\u226513 appears small or sign-flipped)."
    ]
  },
